arch=arm64
vmlinux_btf=6.8 kernel BTF dump (bpftool btf dump file /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux format c, Ubuntu aarch64, 6.8.0-139-generic)
clang=Ubuntu clang version 18.1.3 (1ubuntu1)
load_verified=bpftool prog loadall, Ubuntu aarch64 6.8.0-139-generic, all 15 programs pinned
  (kprobe__mnt_want_write_file is the new one). Then the engine's own kernel
  tests, built and run on that host against this object: leak, drops, filter,
  skip and paths all pass, paths ALL OK over its bind-mount fixtures. NOT
  re-run on the legacy matrix the predecessor was checked against: AlmaLinux
  8.10 (4.18.0-553), CentOS Linux 8.5 (4.18.0-348), Amazon Linux 2 (5.10),
  Amazon Linux 2023 (6.1), Ubuntu 20.04 (5.15 HWE), openSUSE Leap 16.0 (6.12).
notes=rebuilt for RT_ABI_MAJOR 2 (variable-length record, doc 34 F4) plus the
  mnt_want_write_file probe and the ATTR_FILE mount override (F1) and the
  admission hoisting (F8). 15 programs and 9 program sections, against the 14
  and 8 of the object it replaces.
  The header moved from 6.12 (Amazon Linux 2023) to 6.8 (Ubuntu). It keeps the
  property that mattered: this 6.8 struct renamedata still has new_dir and no
  new_parent, so compiling against it keeps exercising the
  bpf_core_field_exists() fallback the x86 object's 7.0 header cannot reach.
  Verified in that host's BTF before building, not assumed.
  Built on the x86_64 workstation rather than on the aarch64 host, which has no
  clang: a BPF object is architecture-independent ELF, so the header and
  -D__TARGET_ARCH_arm64 are the only arch-dependent inputs. Same clang version
  as the x86 object.
  BPF LSM is NOT active on that host (/sys/kernel/security/lsm reports
  lockdown,capability,landlock,yama,apparmor), so the lsm/ programs LOAD but
  the tests exercised the kprobe rung of the fallback ladder.
